{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:bayes","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:219e66e523e67997aa1ba41844a13180f8009a9f7cedf9425b2e7e66dbc1ed92","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.32},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"PRIOR: Historian's 18, from marginal A=0.35 (60/40 weight on the inflation-targeting-era window class over the full 1960- class) and P(B|A)=0.52.\n\nCONJUNCT A (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core >=2.50%/yr avg 2027-36). Prior 0.35. EVIDENCE, Scout: OECD core \"remained stable at 3.6% for the third consecutive month\" and headline 4.3% y/y Aug 2026 (OECD Consumer prices, 6 Oct 2026); euro-area core 2.5% Sept 2026 up from 2.4%; euro-area headline flash \"up 0.6 p.p. to 3.8% - its highest level since September 2023\"; energy inflation \"stabilised above 10%\". CAVEAT PRESERVED VERBATIM FROM SCOUT: the OECD-wide 3.6% is inflated by Turkey and the GDP-weighted OECD-38 core is \"materially lower, and I estimate it at ~2.8-3.1% now — that estimate is MINE, not published, and is the single weakest link in my chain\". LR = P(GDP-weighted core ~2.8-3.1% today with energy >10% | 10-yr avg >=2.50%) / P(same | <2.50%) = 0.75/0.30 = 2.5, above the bar because the mechanism is arithmetic rather than inferential: a hot 2027-29 near-locks one third of the averaging window, leaving 2030-36 needing only (25.0-8.7)/7 = 2.33%, barely above target. I DISCOUNT to 2.0 for the unpublished-weighting caveat. Posterior odds 0.538 x 2.0 = 1.077 -> A = 0.52. CROSS-CHECK as independent corroboration, not prior: Fermi's additive build off a 2.00% anchor (+0.20 care/construction Baumol, +0.25 fiscal-dominance tolerance as net interest goes 2%->3% of GDP, +0.25 non-tradable labour scarcity, +0.08 grid pass-through, -0.09 AI tradable-services deflation,